components.concept:
Time zero on one process model:
- procedures start in no defined order, so a blocking reset written at t = 0 can miss the x->0 negedge;
- declaration initializers are set before any procedure starts (IEEE 1800 vs the 1364-2005 behaviour);
- always_comb runs once at time zero, while always @* waits for a change.
This item also owns the shared process-model core that RunEndingExplorer and the three IPC items build on.

One data-driven classification drill where every option explains itself. Each deck sorts scenarios into bins:
- verification method;
- SystemVerilog feature;
- which standard defines a UVM name, and which compile-stack layer is missing;
- IPC tool;
- what a function body may contain;
- task or function;
- whether a regression log can be trusted.

components.spec:
New src/components/visuals/ScenarioSorter.tsx generalizes the pattern.

PacketSorterGame.tsx becomes a thin wrapper, `<ScenarioSorter deck='containers' testIdPrefix='packet' />`. It keeps its heading, roles, text and data, and still exports PACKET_SORTER_CHALLENGES (re-exported from the deck file). tests/components/PacketSorterGame.test.tsx therefore passes unchanged; it is deliberately not in this item's files.

Props:
- deck (required): 'containers' | 'verification-methods' | 'sv-features' | 'uvm-name-layers' | 'uvm-compile-triage' | 'ipc-tools' | 'function-body' | 'task-or-function' | 'regression-log';
- cards?: string[] (a subset by card id);
- title?;
- testIdPrefix? (default 'sorter').
Integrator: new MDX tag ScenarioSorter.

Per card:
1. Show the prompt and the bin buttons (real <button>s at least 40 px tall, wrapping at 390 px; no drag).
2. The learner commits; this is the PREDICT step.
3. Show feedback for the chosen bin and for the correct bin or bins.
4. Next.
Cards that need a combination take a primary and a secondary bin. A final summary marks each pick correct, partly correct or wrong. Decks with an axis also show the split: sv-features by design half vs verification half; regression-log on its 2x2.

After commit, sv-features cards show a small static anchor picture (fidelity: illustration) on one shared Packet example:
- constraint: dots only inside [1:99];
- coverage: low/high buckets filling;
- assertion: attempt windows on req/gnt;
- class: one driver blueprint used by two tests;
- package/interface: one typedef shared by RTL and TB.

Decks:

verification-methods (8 cards):
- 'never two grants in one cycle' -> formal;
- 'bring up one transaction' -> directed;
- 'long mixed traffic with back-pressure' -> constrained-random;
- 'rare bin stuck at 0 after 1,000 seeds' -> directed or formal;
- 'boot an OS before tape-out' -> emulation;
- 'netlist matches RTL' -> equivalence checking;
- 'async FIFO pointer crossing' -> CDC analysis;
- 'retention register after power-down' -> power-aware simulation.

sv-features. Cards:
- every burst length 1-16 exercised -> covergroup;
- gnt within 3 cycles on every clock -> assertion;
- only legal bursts -> constraint;
- reuse one driver across projects -> class;
- catch an accidental latch -> always_comb, with the caveat 'tools should warn (9.2.2.2), not a guarantee';
- share types between RTL and TB -> package.
Bins: class, constraint, covergroup, assertion, logic, always_comb, interface, package. Wrong-option feedback targets 'a constraint makes values legal but records nothing' and 'an assertion fails on a rule but counts nothing'.

uvm-name-layers. Tiles: class, randomize(), covergroup, logic, $display, uvm_component, uvm_test, uvm_pkg, `uvm_component_utils, run_test, `UVM_VERSION_POST_2017, uvm_macros.svh, `UVM_POST_VERSION_1_2. Bins: IEEE 1800-2023 language / IEEE 1800.2-2020 API / uvm-core 2020.3.1 only. Each tile reveals its defining clause or file.

uvm-compile-triage (the critic's missing triage mode). Symptoms, shown with generic messages labelled tool-dependent:
- unknown type uvm_test;
- unresolved package uvm_pkg;
- undefined macro `uvm_component_utils;
- a method renamed after moving from UVM 1.2.
Bins are compile-stack layers: simulator / uvm-core compiled / `include "uvm_macros.svh" / import uvm_pkg::* / your classes. The reveal draws the stack with the missing layer struck out.

ipc-tools (8 situations, aligned with the lesson table at L381-386). Bins: named event / semaphore / mailbox / bounded mailbox (picking the bound is a second step) / level flag or shared variable.
1. tell the checker, already waiting, that reset was released -> event;
2. tell three monitors that reset was released when one starts waiting at 50 ns, after the release -> level flag with wait;
3. a driver and an interrupt handler share one bus -> semaphore new(1);
4. three threads append to one log file -> semaphore new(1);
5. hand every observed transaction to the checker, in order, none lost -> mailbox;
6. the generator may run at most two items ahead of the driver -> bounded mailbox, bound 2;
7. the scoreboard needs only the latest configuration value -> shared variable;
8. announce 'last item sent' to a checker waiting since time 0 -> event.

function-body. Chips: #10, @(posedge clk), wait(rdy), fork...join, fork...join_any, `fork #5 ready = 1; join_none`, wait fork, write_word(8'h35) (a task), flag <= 1, -> done, $display, void'(max2(3,7)). Bins: legal / illegal in a function body. The two scheduled-later cases (flag <= 1 and the join_none) are shown as 'legal, provided the calling thread comes from an initial or always procedure or a fork in one, in a context where side effects are allowed; otherwise the tool shall issue an error (13.4.4)'.

task-or-function. Jobs from the lesson table: expected value; wait for a handshake; print a summary; return two zero-time results. Bins: nonvoid function / void function / task.

regression-log. Six endings, placed on a 2x2 of 'a ^PASS=\d+ FAIL=0$ matcher accepts it' x 'the run really passed':
- a clean run;
- PASS=0 FAIL=0 without the guard;
- a crash with no summary;
- '# PASS=14 FAIL=0' with a tool prefix;
- an RTL assertion $error beside PASS=14 FAIL=0;
- exit status 0 after $fatal.

components.concept:
Expression rules at bit level, as new families and trap scenarios:
- x-merging in ?: vs if/else;
- bit-count functions with x ($onehot);
- concatenation as an assignment target, and hand sign-extension;
- size and sign casts, unsigned part-selects, literal signedness, and >>> inside an unsigned context;
- the size-cast fix for the lost carry.
It also owns the expression model: it fixes the fill literals and exports the per-node annotations ExpressionTreeExplorer and LiteralDecoder need.

Aggregate types at bit level:
- a packed struct is one vector with the first member most significant, and a narrower assignment zero-extends silently;
- unpacked structs have no bit numbering;
- hard packed unions need members of equal size, while soft packed unions right-justify and leave the upper bits alone;
- a tagged union guards its reads with the tag and has a defined packed layout;
- a mutation board shows which self-checks catch which change;
- a clinic covers the classic struct and enum bugs.

How a delay becomes a wake time and how a time value reaches the log:
- the time unit scales a delay and the element's own precision rounds it;
- which `timescale or timeunit applies to which element;
- the global precision and 1step;
- $time, $stime, $realtime, %t and $timeformat;
- time values that lose their unit when they cross a scope.

components.spec:
New component on a new pure model. All arithmetic is exact: delays are parsed as decimal strings into integer femtoseconds held in BigInt, with half-tick detection by doubling. Never floats. Build after OutcomeGroups (for ReorderList). Integrator: MDX tag TimescaleLab.

Props: mode 'rounding' (default) | 'two-modules' | 'read-time' | 'which-unit' | 'global-precision' | 'cross-scope'; preset?: string.

rounding:
- Pick a unit and a precision (1, 10 or 100 of s, ms, us, ns, ps, fs); a precision coarser than the unit is rejected with 3.14.
- Type #d or a time literal (#2.1ns) and PREDICT the wake time.
- The reveal is a ruler of precision ticks with the scaled delay snapping to a tick.
- Presets:
  - the six lesson table rows (L332-337);
  - #1.55 at 1ns/100ps (the spine's assessment item): 1.6 ns;
  - #2.1ns at 10ns/1ns: 2 ns;
  - #0.4 at 1ns/1ns: same slot;
  - #2.85 at 1ns/100ps;
  - #(x);
  - #(-1).

Tie handling (critic point resolved): at an exact half tick the model computes both round-half-away-from-zero and round-half-to-even.
- When they agree, it shows one answer. 2.75 ns gives 27.5 ticks, which is 28 under both rules, so the standard's 2.8 ns; 15.5 ns gives 16 ns under both; 1.55 ns gives 1.6 ns under both.
- When they disagree (#2.85: 28.5 ticks, 29 vs 28), it shows both values, labelled 'tool-dependent: 3.14.1 states no tie rule'. This matches the lesson's own Expert note (L704).
- The draft's blanket 'every half step is tool-dependent' rule is dropped, because it would have contradicted the standard's own examples.

two-modules:
- tb_top (1ns/100ps, ● marker) and u_slow (10ns/1ns, ◆ marker) each run the same #d, 1-3 times from t = 0, on one absolute 0-30 ns axis. Default d = 1.26, editable.
- PREDICT each wake time first. Defaults: 1.3 ns; 13 ns, then 26 ns.
- A footer shows the global precision (100 ps) and that each module rounds with its own precision.

read-time:
- Choose the caller's unit and precision and a delay sequence. PREDICT $time, $stime, $realtime and the %t text.
- The reveal shows three aligned rulers: absolute time, caller units, and the integer $time.
- $timeformat controls: units -15..2 from Table 20-2, precision digits, suffix, minimum width; or 'not called' for the Table 20-3 defaults. When the build has timeunit but no `timescale, the default units are shown as 'tool-dependent'.
- Toggle %t of $time vs $realtime to watch the fraction disappear.
- Presets:
  - '20.3.1' (#1.55 twice at 10ns/1ns: $time 2 then 3, $realtime 1.6 then 3.2);
  - the Make It Work two-module bench;
  - the UVM '@ 15000' stamp;
  - 'f2d-printing-time' (F2D's lines).

which-unit:
- A ReorderList of compile units:
  - a.sv with `timescale 1ns/1ps;
  - b.sv with none;
  - c.sv with timeunit 100ps;
  - a package with none;
  - an optional `resetall;
  - an optional compilation-unit timeunit;
  - a nested module.
- A switch: 'one compilation unit' / 'each file its own'.
- PREDICT b's unit. Every element is then tagged with its resolved unit and precision, the precedence rule (a-d) that decided it, any mixed-specification error (labelled 'an error per the standard; some tools only warn'), and the resulting global precision.
- The implementation default is always shown as 'implementation-specific', never as a number.

global-precision:
- Add 'vendor model compiled under `timescale 1ns/1fs'.
- PREDICT $printtimescale($root), 1step and the default %t units: all become 1 fs, while every module's own rounding is unchanged.

cross-scope (the lesson's debug challenge, exact code):
- util_pkg (timeunit 1ps; timeprecision 1ps; task automatic wait_t(time t); #t;) and tb_top (timeunit 1ns; timeprecision 1ps; $timeformat(-9, 3, " ns", 0)).
- Call forms and the PREDICT wake time for each:
  - wait_t(5ns): 5 ps, printing 'woke at 0.005 ns';
  - wait_t(1.5ns): the real 1.5 rounds to 2 when passed to time, so 2 ps;
  - the fix wait_ns(5) with #(delay_ns * 1ns): 5000 ps = 5 ns;
  - repeat (n) @(posedge clk): unit-free.
- An animation step shows the value crossing the scope boundary and dropping its unit tag.
- Suspects: the `#t` line, the wait_t call, the tb_top timeunit line (decoy), the $timeformat line (decoy: it changes only printing).
- Fixes: the scaled wait_ns, a project-wide timeunit, passing a cycle count.
- The model grades the suspect and the fix by recomputing the wake time. HintLadder with three hints.

Keyboard operable; rulers scroll inside their panel at 390 px; light and dark tokens.

components.concept:
Where a task's or function's variables live:
- overlapping static vs automatic activations;
- recursion, which needs automatic;
- when the caller sees a change for each argument direction (copy-in, copy-out at return, ref);
- static initializers;
- the directions and types a formal inherits when you do not write them;
- a kata that debugs three subroutine bugs.

Which item a decision statement runs and what it reports:
- the case family and if-else-if chains, plain or qualified with unique, unique0 or priority, with and without default or a final else;
- case inside, with its asymmetric wildcard matching and 'x means no match', compared side by side with casex.

components.spec:
New component on a new pure decision model. Integrator: MDX tag DecisionLab.

Overlap with LogicStateDiagram, resolved: flow-control L53-74 keeps LogicStateDiagram's case panel. The lesson text there names its items (A 2'b00, B 2'b1?, C 2'bx1, default D), so content depends on it. DecisionLab does not repeat that drill. It owns:
- qualifier reports and the 'no default, nothing runs' rule (L76-208);
- the asymmetric case inside comparison (L210-237).
Both components call the same caseItemMatches from sv-four-state-model, so they cannot disagree.

Props: mode 'qualifiers' (default) | 'compare'; preset?: string.

qualifiers mode:
- Pick the form (case, casez, casex, case inside, if-else-if), the qualifier (plain, unique, unique0, priority) and default/final-else on or off.
- Edit the select bits (0/1/x/z) or load a preset:
  - the lesson decoder 2'b00/2'b01/2'b10 with sel 01, 11, xx (L101-110 table);
  - casez 2'b1? / 2'b?1 with sel 11;
  - unique if with req 10, 11, 00 (L120-126 table);
  - one-hot unique case (1'b1) with gnt 011;
  - the priority casez encoder with req 0110 and 0000;
  - the standard's bit [2:0] a examples: unique case reports for 3, 5, 6, 7; priority casez 3'b00? / 3'b0?? reports for 4-7; unique0 never reports;
  - Kata C steps 1-4;
  - the standard's priority case (status) inside with items `1, 3` and `3'b0?0, [4:7]`: 'b0x0 matches, while 'b00x and 'bxxx report.
- Before the reveal, PREDICT two things: which item runs, and what is reported (nothing / no match / overlap).
- The reveal:
  - marks every item: matches and runs / matches but not run (the first match wins) / no match;
  - shows a violation ticket with its clause and the note 'checked now, reported in the Observed region (12.4.2.1, 12.5.3.1)'. It names no other component; the integrator may link DeferredReportVisualizer once it exists;
  - highlights the live cell of a qualifier x {no match, one match, two or more} grid (the lesson's L181 figure made live).
- A plain case with no default and no match reads 'nothing runs, nothing reported'.

compare mode:
- One select, two columns: casex | case inside. Edit the sel bits and the item set (2'b10/2'b11, or 2'b1?).
- PREDICT the branch in each column.
- The reveal shows per-bit ==? chips (1/0/x), the OR across an item's comparisons, and an 'x means no match' stamp.
- Preset 'opcode-decoder' (operators lesson): the case inside column uses the lesson's items exactly (3'b000; 3'b001, 3'b010; [3'b100:3'b110]; 3'b11?; default).
  - A range item is legal only in case inside, so the casex column is labelled 'written as casex with plain items' and lists the range as three items 3'b100, 3'b101, 3'b110. This matches the lesson's own 'written as casex (op) with plain items' remark.
  - All six trace values and 3'b0x0 are pinned by tests: 3'b0x0 runs NOP under casex and reaches default under case inside; 3'b1x0 reaches default under case inside.
  - A casez toggle shows z don't-cares.

Every lesson table at flow-control L101-124 becomes a model test. Keyboard operable, 390 px, PredictionPrompt with per-option feedback.

New component on a new pure cycle model. Build after OutcomeGroups; it uses MutationMatrix. Integrator: MDX tag SelfCheckBenchLab.

Ownership of mutation matrices (critic point): each bench lab owns its own DUT model and mutants. This lab owns adder_reg + tb_top; CounterBenchLab owns the F1B counter. Both draw with the shared MutationMatrix. The F2E 'regression-log' question (can a log be trusted?) is a different skill and stays a ScenarioSorter deck.

Props: mode 'step' (default) | 'matrix' | 'debug'; preset?: 'smoke' | 'corners' | 'gaps' | 'all' | 'hold'; bug?: 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 'extra-stage' | 'swap-ab'; focus?: 'anatomy'.

step mode:
- Pick a test preset and a DUT (BUG=0/1/2), then step edge by edge with PlaybackControls (arrow keys).
- PredictionPrompts gate two reveals: 'On which edge does the checker see sum = 10?' and 'What does exp_q hold after edge 6?'.
- From one model step it then shows:
  - a CycleWaveform of clk, rst_n, in_valid, a, b, out_valid, sum;
  - the exp_q strip, animating push_back and pop_front;
  - pass_cnt and fail_cnt;
  - the log (TEST, CHK, PASS=n FAIL=m);
  - the highlighted tb_top line (send, checker, check_result, drain, report). Code is generated from the model data.
- The anatomy pane is a BlockDiagram copy of the lesson's L71 diagram. The node active at the step lights up, and one transaction token moves from send() through both paths (DUT and model_add -> exp_q) to the checker. Clicking a node jumps to its code line.

matrix mode:
- A MutationMatrix with rows smoke, corners, gaps, hold and columns:
  - BUG=1, carry stuck at 0;
  - BUG=2, off by one;
  - BUG=3, sum moves on idle cycles;
  - an extra pipeline stage;
  - a and b swapped (an equivalent mutant).
- The learner predicts escapes / fails per cell. Each cell then shows its real summary line, run by the model.
- Clicking a cell opens that run in the stepper at its first failing edge, or explains the escape (for example: no smoke result reaches 256, so bit 8 is 0 whether or not the carry is broken).
- A toggle adds the kata's hold check, showing BUG=3 needs both test_hold and the check.
- The swap column explains that no test can kill an equivalent mutant.

debug mode, broken benches:
- reset_dut() deleted: rst_n x, every check skipped, PASS=0 FAIL=0 before the guard;
- an X that passes through !=;
- a check made one edge early (each actual equals the previous expected);
- one expected variable that the next send() overwrites;
- `logic clk;` with no initializer (WDOG fatal);
- a missing final idle() (unexpected result);
- a missing $finish (the summary prints, but the run never ends).
Flow: read the model-generated log; click the suspect tb_top line; choose a fix; the model reruns and grades the fix. Diagnostic feedback for every wrong suspect; HintLadder with three hints. A fix passes only if the run ends PASS=<n> FAIL=0 and BUG=1 is still caught.

At 390 px the waveform and queue scroll inside their panels.

components.concept:
Which region the 4.5 loop moves to next, the reactive set draining first, and clocking-block drive timing. This item owns the scheduler model and adds what three other items need:
- inversion;
- a resume trigger and a slot time;
- concurrent-assertion processes with $sampled;
- $finish and the implicit program $finish;
- trace-only violation-check statements;
- an opt-in NBA interleave choice.

components.spec:
Build after SVSchedulerRegionVisualizer, which adds inv, the resume trigger, slotTime and interleaveNba to the model. Every existing preset keeps working; tests stay in tests/components/scheduler-visuals.test.tsx. The draft's tests/components/TimeSlotTraceVisualizer.test.tsx does not exist, so no duplicate file is created. Integrator: no new tag.

TimeSlotTraceVisualizer gets regionView?: 'ladder' (default) | 'folded', drawn by the new FoldedSlotView.tsx.

The folded view draws:
- one 'this time slot' box with alternating ◆ update and ▶ evaluation events;
- value chips for the watched signals;
- a Δ counter, labelled 'model count';
- a $time readout from slotTime.

New presets in sv-scheduler-scenarios.ts:
(1) 'f3a-one-edge', built from F3A L184-201 exactly:
- a clock process with trigger 'resume' running clk = ~clk, its next #5 noted as 'books t = 10';
- `always_ff @(posedge clk) q <= d;`;
- `assign q_n = ~q;`;
- an initial woken by @(posedge clk) (the critic's fix: not #5) that runs $display, then $strobe, then '#1 books t = 6'.
Initial values: clk 0, d 1, q 0, q_n 1; slotTime 5.
PREDICT both printed lines ('5 display q=0 q_n=1', '5 strobe  q=1 q_n=0'), then step. A 'Scheduler choice' toggle swaps steps 3 and 4 (always_ff vs initial) and shows the output does not change.
A test asserts the trace equals the lesson's eight-event table (L219-228) step for step:
- ▶ clock;
- ◆ clk 0->1 waking always_ff and the initial;
- ▶ always_ff;
- ▶ initial;
- ◆ q 0->1;
- ▶ assign;
- ◆ q_n 1->0;
- end of slot with the $strobe print.
On a mismatch, report to the integrator and never change content.
(2) 'f3a-one-edge-zero-delay' ('Show advanced'): #0 before $display. PREDICT 'does it now print q=1?' It still prints q=0, because Inactive comes before NBA.
(3) 'comb-after-nba': registers the existing combAfterNbaScenario. A PredictionPrompt gates the final y and z, the Δ count, and 'does $time advance?' (no).
(4) 'program-drive': registers programReactiveScenario, whose Re-NBA write of cmd wakes the design assign in Active after the NBA pass. Δ goes past 1; the test pins the model's Δ against the count of move-to-Active steps, never a hand-written number.
(5) 'gclk-interleave':
- gclk is generated with <= (`gclk <= ~gclk`) and `a <= 1` is pending in the same NBA batch; `always @(posedge gclk) b <= a;`.
- With interleaveNba on, OutcomeGroups-style results show two groups: b captures the old a or the new a.
- 'gclk-enable' replaces the generated clock with a real clk and an enable: one group.
- PREDICT 'old a, new a, or either?' first.

RaceConditionDebugger gets bench?: 'shift' (default) | 'handoff' | 'nba-writers'. nba-writers has a toggle between 'one process: `a <= 0; a <= 1;` in one begin-end' and 'two processes woken by one edge: `a <= 0` and `a <= 1`'. PREDICT 'defined or not defined?', then run every legal order:
- one process: a = 1 every time (4.6);
- two processes: two outcome groups, even with <= (4.7).
This replaces the draft's TimeSlotTraceVisualizer presets 'nba-same-process' / 'nba-two-writers', which overlapped RaceConditionDebugger's existing =/<= exploration.

RegionLadder and TraceNarration are imported unchanged.

components.concept:
Keyword sets are chosen per region of source text by `begin_keywords/`end_keywords:
- each version specifier reserves a cumulative set, and noconfig removes the configuration words;
- pairs nest as a stack across files, and `resetall does not pop them;
- with no directive, the set is the implementation's default;
- an escaped keyword is an ordinary identifier.
A lineage mode ties each standard revision to the keyword table it added. The item also fixes VerilogVsSystemVerilog's no-directive prompt.

A self-checking test is evidence only if it can fail. Bench-side silent failures:
- a missing or disabled compare;
- a self-copy;
- an X-blind !=.
Mutation testing proves that a check can fail. Code coverage counts what ran and functional coverage counts planned scenarios; neither checks anything.

components.modelRules:
IEEE 1800-2023:
- != with x/z operands gives 1'bx (11.4.5), and if treats x as false, taking else (12.4); !== always gives 0 or 1 (11.4.5).
- 40.2.2 defines statement coverage (a statement executed at least once), toggle coverage (each bit seen at 0 and at 1) and FSM coverage only. Label branch and condition coverage 'tool-defined', and keep the lesson's note that some tools count transitions for toggle.

The model (counter-bench-model.ts):
- uses simulateCounter with its rst schedule and nextCount (X per 12.4) from counter-bug-model, and compare/ifBranch from sv-four-state-model;
- maps check i to the value after rising edge i.

Tests reproduce the lesson exactly:
- correct DUT: PASS=20 FAIL=0 seen_max=2;
- WRAP_AT(6): PASS=7 FAIL=13 seen_max=0, first failure at 80 ns;
- stuck-X DUT with !=: PASS=20 FAIL=0. Note: debug-challenge item 4 has no else, so it counts nothing;
- stuck-X DUT with !==: FAIL=20;
- coverage counts: always_ff 20, rst 1, wrap 2, increment 17.
Report disagreements to the integrator.

components.concept:
The read-call contract on a character stream:
- $fopen returns 0 on failure;
- $fscanf skips white space and returns the number of items assigned: 0 on an early mismatch, -1 only when the input ends before the first conversion;
- $feof is nonzero only after a read has hit the end;
- with %h %h %h, a short line takes its missing field from the next line.
Plusarg matching follows the same kind of contract. Includes the plusarg-selected, file-driven bench and three of the 'log that lies' cases.

What each access does to an associative array:
- a plain read allocates nothing and warns;
- assignment, a ref actual and a read-modify-write allocate;
- a declared default changes what a missing read returns.
Also: queue slices and indexed writes; a first()/next() walk that deletes as it goes; and predicting a queue after a mixed op sequence.

Why an X result slips past a check:
- != returns x, and if treats x as false and takes the else branch, so if (!=)/else counts the X as a pass;
- a 2-state copy turns x into 0;
- !== and assert(==) catch it;
- an x out_valid can skip the compare entirely.
The F2A debug challenge shows that two independent mechanisms hide one x.

Which value changes are events and when an assignment's value is read and written:
- the posedge/negedge table with x/z, and the LSB rule;
- operand changes that are not events;
- or lists and iff guards;
- wait passing at once when the level is already true;
- intra-assignment delays and repeat event controls (Table 9-3).

components.spec:
New component on a new pure decision function. Build after OutcomeGroups, for ReorderList. Integrator: MDX tag FixPathDecisionLab.

Props: mode 'decide' (default) | 'kata'; preset?: string.

decide:
- Presets are the four worked-example bugs:
  - a wrong reset value 3 weeks before tape-out;
  - > vs >= with spare cells nearby;
  - a new pipeline stage of several hundred flip-flops;
  - field corruption with a defeature bit.
- Custom mode exposes the facts as toggles: taped out yes/no; needs new transistors yes/no; gates needed vs spare cells nearby; timing closes yes / no / unknown; firmware or defeature path available.
- The learner first commits a prediction of the production fix. Option feedback diagnoses 'lines of RTL = layers', 'FIB fixes production' and 'any bug can be fixed in metal'.
- The reveal shows:
  1. the lesson SVG's three-band layer cross-section (hatched = new mask, solid = reused or held wafer, dashed = no mask yet);
  2. the triage decision tree, with the path taken highlighted as steps 2-4 of 'How to triage';
  3. the rule-of-thumb multiplier and stage, read from bug-cost-model;
  4. a qualitative delay class: days to weeks / weeks / months / 'workaround as fast as a firmware release; the real fix waits for new silicon';
  5. an escape-analysis line naming the earlier activity that should have caught the bug.
- If timing is unknown, the result is 'depends': metal-only if physical design finds a timing-clean fix, otherwise a full respin. Kata 3 needs exactly this.

kata:
- The four kata scenarios:
